<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><!-- Original-type: ra--><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">11720475</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2375252</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6692101</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1054/bjoc.2001.2101</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Reduced expression of <italic>BAX</italic> is associated with poor prognosis in patients with epithelial ovarian cancer: a multifactorial analysis of <italic>TP53</italic>, <italic>p21</italic>, <italic>BAX</italic> and <italic>BCL-2</italic></article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Schuyer</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Burg</surname><given-names>M E L van der</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Henzen-Logmans</surname><given-names>S C</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Fieret</surname><given-names>J H</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Klijn</surname><given-names>J G M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Look</surname><given-names>M P</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Foekens</surname><given-names>J A</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Stoter</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Berns</surname><given-names>E M J J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Medical Oncology (Division of Endocrine Oncology)</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>Department of Pathology, University Hospital Rotterdam/DDHK, The Netherlands</aff><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>Author for correspondence: </corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>01</day><month>09</month><year>2001</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>11</month><year>2001</year></pub-date><volume>85</volume><issue>9</issue><fpage>1359</fpage><lpage>1367</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>03</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>16</day><month>07</month><year>2001</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>07</day><month>08</month><year>2001</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 2001, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2001</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>Traditional clinicopathological features do not predict which patients will develop chemotherapy resistance. The <italic>TP53</italic> gene is frequently altered in ovarian cancer but its prognostic implications are controversial. Little is known on the impact of TP53-downstream genes on prognosis. Using molecular and immunohistochemical analyses we examined <italic>TP53</italic> and its downstream genes <italic>p21</italic> <italic>BAX</italic> and <italic>BCL-2</italic> in ovarian tumour tissues and have evaluated the results in relation to clinico-pathological parameters, clinical outcome and response to platinum-based chemotherapy. Associations of tested factors and patient and tumour characteristics were studied by Spearman rank correlation and Pearsons &#x003c7;<sup>2</sup> test. The Cox proportional hazard model was used for univariate and multivariate analysis. The associations of tested factors with response was tested using logistic regression analysis. <italic>TP53</italic> mutation, p21 and BCL-2 expression were not associated with increased rates of progression and death. Expression of TP53 was associated with a shorter overall survival only (relative hazard rate [RHR] 2.01 <italic>P</italic> = 0.03). Interestingly, when combining <italic>TP53</italic> mutation and expression data, this resulted in an increased association with overall survival (<italic>P</italic> = 0.008). BAX expression was found to be associated with both progression-free (RHR 0.44 <italic>P</italic> = 0.05) and overall survival (RHR 0.42 <italic>P</italic> = 0.03). Those patients who simultaneously expressed BAX and BCL-2 had a longer progression-free and overall survival compared to patients whose tumours did not express BCL-2 (<italic>P</italic> = 0.05 and 0.015 respectively). No relations were observed between tested factors and response to platinum-based chemotherapy. We conclude that BAX expression may represent a prognostic indicator for patients with ovarian cancer and that the combined evaluation of <italic>BAX</italic> and <italic>BCL-2</italic> may provide additional prognostic significance. &#x02002;&#x02002;http://www.bjcancer.com &#x000a9; 2001 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>ovarian cancer</kwd><kwd>prognosis</kwd><kwd><italic>TP53</italic></kwd><kwd><italic>BAX</italic></kwd><kwd><italic>BCL-2</italic></kwd><kwd><italic>p21</italic></kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>